Why This Integrates Everything We Discussed
Extracts multi‐digit strings (3–8 length by default) from each R2/R4/R6/R8 cell.

3‐value check: We skip any substring that has more than 3 distinct digits in normal or more than 3 in V-Trac.

Highlights them in the HTML table so you visually confirm the script sees the same “clusters” you do.

Scores each pattern with a straightforward weighting:

vertical coverage = how many of {R2,R4,R6,R8} found it,

adjacency = how many consecutive columns it occupies,

mirror bonus if the substring has a digit & its mirror pair,

consensus if it’s a full 4‐row coverage in col=1.

Groups by (section,Set,Draw,Col,canonical_form) to unify permutations (e.g. “468,” “864,” “648” are the same canonical pattern).

Outputs a single final scoreboard in CSV sorted by score.

Once you see the results, you can tweak these parts:

is_3value_pattern(sub) – if you want to allow up to 4 digits, or require strictly ≤3 normal digits plus ≤2 V‐Trac, etc.

min_len/max_len – if you want to allow bigger stable substrings (like 12 digits).

Scoring – inside the loop at base_score = rowcov*2 + best_run + .... Feel free to add or remove factors (hot‐zone detection, horizontal “straight” detection, etc.).

By default, it doesn’t check “hot zone” marks ('*') or multi‐day “lingering” bonuses. If you want them, you can see how easy it is to add a line in the scoring loop:

python
Copy
hot = 0
# e.g. if any row in that draw/col has '*'
# you'd do something like:  if '*' in some cell => hot=2
base_score += hot
Similarly, for lingering across sets, you’d track (set_name, draw, col, cform) in a dictionary. That’s the nice part about everything being in one Python file: you can expand it without rummaging in 4–5 different scripts.

That’s it – a monolithic “Stable Pattern Extractor” that should “just work” in Cursor. Copy–paste the script above, run it, and open the resulting HTML. Now you can visually see if the recognized patterns align with your manual picks – and adjust from there. Have fun!
